countries are in the grip of an energy  crisis with gas prices up 300 percent  since the outbreak of war in Ukraine and  EU leaders are still gripped by  indecision over how to deal with it this  road map that now has to be worked on  and then implemented it is a joint  purchase big step forward it is the  rules for sharing gas so the solidarity  agreements and the rules that have to be  followed in case of a severe disruption  it is the topic of excessive price  prices and how to limit them  despite hints at agreement division  remains between heavyweights Germany and  France on an energy price cap that would  see the EU refusing to trade gas above a  certain level Germany believes that  would lead to supply shortages with  Traders simply selling their Wares on  Asian markets for a higher price they've  agreed to keep talking  in classic EU style the leaders have  pushed discussions onto energy ministers  who'll meet next week ahead of a  possible emergency Summit next month it  seems likely they'll agree to some form  of cartel style gas buying similar to  the methods used to buy vaccines during  the pandemic it is less certain to what  extent they may agree on a price cap  foreign  substantial time was given to Ukraine  itself ways of helping to protect its  energy infrastructure the target of  repeated Russian attack  Ukraine's President Vladimir zielinski  addressed the leaders via video link he  warned that Russia was planning further  attacks on energy hubs in particular a  hydroelectric dam in the south  if the Russian terrorists blow out this  Dam more than 80 settlements including  her song will face rapid flooding  hundreds of thousands of people could be  affected  ahead of a freezing winter he warned  part of Russia's plan is to set off  another wave of migration towards the EU  Jonah Al Jazeera Brussels
 

